  "notes": [
   "The parts list is unchanged from step 1. Step 1 was correct.",
   "The catalogue card for arduino-due lists no digital pin 2, pin 3 or A0 port. The tutorial needs them, so I used the port names '2', '3' and 'A0' on due1 as the Uno-style naming does. Please check them against the real Due port list.",
   "The Due card has a single GND port, so one GND wire is used. The 3.3V pin is the 3V3 port.",
   "The breadboard column numbers and rail hole numbers are my own layout choice. The image does not give exact holes. Every strip connection follows the tutorial text.",
   "Each button has side 1 on the top bank (tied to 3.3V through the red wire from the power rail). Side 2 is on the bottom bank. The Due pin wire and the 10k pull-down resistor to the ground rail share that side-2 strip. This follows the text: the input pin and the pull-down are on the same leg, and the other leg goes to 3.3V.",
   "The image draws the red button wires and the resistors near the bottom rails. I could not tell exactly which bank the blue signal wires land in, so I followed the text for the pull-down arrangement.",
   "The top and bottom rails are linked by a red and a black wire at the left. So the 3.3V on the top rail also reaches the bottom power rail, and ground reaches the bottom ground rail used by the resistors.",
   "The potentiometer's outer legs go to 3.3V and ground. Its wiper (SIG) goes to A0 through the yellow wire. The DAC0 and DAC1 outputs are left unconnected because the tutorial only says to probe them with an optional oscilloscope."
  ]
